            <ArticleTitle>[Proceedings: Early mobilization after myocardial infarct--late results].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1457-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The longterm effects of early mobilization after myocardial infarction (MI) have been investigated. 122 patients aged under 65 years were reexamined 84.6 +/- 24 weeks after MI. The physical work capacity (PWC) of these men was 22% below the normal value. Patients with angina pectoris after MI had a PWC only 45% of normal. The overwhelming majority (88%) of MI survivors had returned to their jobs by the end of the follow-up period. Of the conventionally treated patients only 70% had returned to work. Age, PWC after MI and psychological status critically influence the rapidity and rate of return to work after MI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
